Z||000000||daveml66||04-04-2001||10:59 AM||||well  i gotta replace my trans.  i was wondering what is the easiest way to remove it???  and  is the 84 4 cyl.  4spd the same as the one on the v6s?????||||reg||5
Z||000001||Formula88||04-04-2001||11:33 AM||||Well, the easiest way is to take it to a transmission shop.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Or you could click here: <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#trany" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#trany</A> <P>I used these instructions when I pulled the tranny out of my Formula for a clutch job.  It's all the info you need.  A shop manual would come in handy, too.  Good luck!  ||||reg||4
Z||000002||Carrolles||04-04-2001||12:42 PM||||I'd drop the cradle (not terribly hard to do).  Then you could take care of other maintenance while the whole thing is out.  Things like porting the intake and exhaust manifolds, welding exhaust manifold cracks, checking vacuum lines, cleaning and touch-up painting, checking timing chain and water pump, etc.<P>None of this costs much and is much easier to do with the engine out.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Will||04-04-2001||03:50 PM||||I've done the clutch change using the cradle tip method. I think that an easier way might be to suspend the engine on a 2x4 between the strut towers and totally remove the cradle, while leaving the engine and transmission in place, then remove the transmission.||||reg||1
Z||000004||daveml66||04-04-2001||11:21 PM||||well i've had the whole cradle out a few weeks ago and did all my engine tuning (wish i'd payed more attention to the trans then) so i don't feel like doing that again. plus i had acess to a lift then. this time i'm gonna try it right in my drive way. thanks for the info.  but  does ne one know if the 84 4spd is as strong as the ones in the in later gts ????????  hmmmm???||||reg||10
